 ## Overview
-Densification Strategy defines the mechanisms for dynamically managing the number and spatial distribution of 3D Gaussian primitives during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Parent]. It addresses the problem of under-representing complex scene details or over-representing empty space by splitting, cloning, or pruning Gaussians based on optimization metrics [Parent]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is the iterative, feedback-driven adaptation of the primitive set throughout the training process, balancing reconstruction quality against computational cost [Parent].
